  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Effective frameworks on employment, protection and rights for migrant workers including returnee migrants are in operation</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Bangladesh is the 6th largest migrant-sending country, with over 650,000 people migrating annually, contributing 4.6% to GDP through remittances. Despite its economic importance, migration governance faces challenges, including high recruitment costs, weak law enforcement, limited awareness of rights, and inadequate protection for workers&#x2014;especially women. Migrants are low- or semi-skilled, leading to lack of better jobs and vulnerability to exploitation. Gender barriers limit women&#x2019;s access to skills training. Reintegration support is insufficient, with returnees unemployed and facing financial hardship. Policies like the Wage Earner Welfare Board Act 2018 exist, but social protection remains fragmented and lacks portability.
ILO&#x2019;s strategy: (a) supports legal and policy reforms on labour migration and promotes the application of international labour standards (ILS), including the UN Convention on Migrant Workers; (b) facilitates talent partnerships with EU countries to create pathways for skilled migration in EU labour markets; (c) supports the Government in building a Reintegration Framework, including policy development, coordination mechanisms, and services to returnees with government and private sector partners; (d) helps, in collaboration with IOM and UN Women, to advance social protection frameworks addressing specific needs of vulnerable groups, ensuring they are protected and empowered throughout the migration cycle. 
Expected results include the establishment of fair recruitment practices rooted in ILS and social dialogue; improved governance to protect migrant rights through ILS and effective bilateral agreements; development of circular skilled migration mechanisms; and the creation of an inclusive, gender-responsive reintegration framework. Furthermore, extending social protection that is portable, inclusive, and aligned with bilateral agreements will support sustainable reintegration and economic resilience for returnee migrants.</narrative>
  </description>
